# The bracket: positions and slices

The **bracket** is the postfix form `xs[index]`. It is one operator whose
index type selects its meaning: an `Int` index reads a **position**, and a
`Range` index takes a **slice**. `a:b` inside the bracket builds a `Range`,
and either bound may be left out.

```hql
sightings = ["barn-owl", "common-raven", "european-robin", "house-sparrow"]

sightings[0]      // some("barn-owl")
sightings[-1]     // some("house-sparrow")
sightings[1:3]    // ["common-raven", "european-robin"]
sightings[-2:]    // ["european-robin", "house-sparrow"]
"hello"[1:3]      // "el"
```

The bracket MUST touch the value it indexes. A bracket after a space or a
line break opens a list literal. `xs[]` is a syntax error; `xs[:]` is the
whole value. The receiver is evaluated first, then the start bound, then the
end bound.

## `Range`

`type Range { start? : Int, end? : Int }` is declared in `lib/std/core/mod.hql` and is
in scope in every program. `xs[a:b]` is `xs[Range(start = a, end = b)]`, and a
bound left out is absent, so a window named once is reused:

```hql
window = Range(start = 1, end = 3)
[sightings[window], "hello"[window]]
```

An index whose type is neither `Int` nor `Range` is a type error, and so is a
bound of `a:b` that is not an `Int`.

## Positions

Positions count from `0` over a length `n`. A negative position `p` denotes
position `p + n`, counted from the end. `xs[i]` is the element at the
resolved position when it lies in `0..n`, and absence otherwise; its type is
`Option<T>` over an element type `T`.

## Slices

A slice resolves each bound as a position is resolved, then clamps it into
`0..=n`. A missing start is `0` and a missing end is `n`. With `lo` and `hi`
the clamped bounds, the slice holds the elements at `lo` up to, not including,
`hi`, and is empty when `lo >= hi`. A slice over a finite value does not fail
on any `Int` bounds.

| Receiver | `xs[i]` | `xs[a:b]` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `String` | `Option<String>`, one character | `String` |
| `List<T>`, `Seq<T>` | `Option<T>` | the receiver's type |
| `Ranking<T>` | `Option<Hit<T>>` | `Ranking<T>` |
| `Data` | `Option<Data>` | `Data` |
| `Option<S>` | `Option` of `S`'s position | `Option<S>`; absence is absence |
| `Stream<T>` | refused, naming `take(n)` | by its bounds, below |
| `Set<T>`, `Map<K, V>` | refused | refused |
| a declared type | through its `Index<T>` | through its `Slice` |

A String's positions count characters, as `length`, `char_at` and `slice` do.
`Data` that is not a sequence is an evaluation error naming its shape.

## Streams

A `Stream<T>` may not end, so the type of its slice depends on the written
bounds. `xs[:b]` is `take(b)` and `xs[a:b]` is `drop(a) | take(b - a)`; both
end, so both are `List<T>`. `xs[a:]` is `drop(a)` and `xs[:]` the Stream
itself, both `Stream<T>`. A negative bound is refused: a literal one at
typecheck, a computed one at evaluation, since a position counted from the end
needs an end. A bounded slice produces at most `b` values. A Stream is sliced
only with a written `a:b`: a `Range` value does not show whether its end is
present.

## The capabilities behind the bracket

A position resolves the core trait `Index<T>` and a slice the core trait
`Slice`, both declared in `lib/std/core/mod.hql`:

```hql
trait Slice {
    fn slice(self, range: Range) : Self
}
```

`Seq`, `String` and `Data` implement both natively; a List and a Ranking take
`Seq`'s. `Data` is a union, so its implementations do not apply to `String`,
one of its alternatives: a String takes its own. A declared type implements
`Slice` for its own values, and its slice is of its own type:

```hql
type Playlist { name : String, tracks : List<String> }

impl Slice for Playlist {
    fn slice(self, range: Range) : Playlist =
        Playlist(name = self.name, tracks = self.tracks[range])
}
```

`Stream` implements neither: its slice is not of its own type. A receiver
with no slicing is a type error naming what is sliced.

The String module keeps `s.slice(start, end)`, which is `s[start:end]`;
`s.slice(range)` with one argument is `Slice`'s. A call selects one of the two
by its argument count.

See [HQL-0026](../../../proposals/HQL-0026/README.md) and the
[slicing corpus](../../../../examples/slicing/README.md).
